{
  "rules": [
    "Strong positive bias: model forecasts are consistently above realized prices, especially during/after downturns. Apply a downward level shift to the whole path (larger in bearish regimes).",
    "Trend/turning-point lag: model is slow to recognize regime breaks; it keeps projecting high/flat prices while the market sells off. Add a regime-switch trigger: if recent returns are negative and volatility rises, accelerate the downward adjustment early in the horizon.",
    "Overshoot after peaks/spikes: when history contains a local high, the model extrapolates elevated levels; true outcomes mean-revert lower. Damp forecasts proportional to recent deviation from a short-term moving average (stronger damping when price is above MA).",
    "Horizon-dependent error growth: overprediction increases with forecast horizon (errors compound). Use a horizon ramp: small correction in days 1–5, larger correction days 6–15, largest correction days 16–30.",
    "Underestimates drawdown depth: model predicts shallow dips and quick rebounds; realized paths show deeper, more persistent declines. Impose a drawdown floor based on recent downside volatility (e.g., subtract k * downside stdev) and slow the rebound speed."
  ]
}
